<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Delhi Durbar</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1877">1877</a>, <a href="/years/1903">1903</a>, <a href="/years/1911">1911</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> Durbars were imperial assemblies held at Delhi to mark the accession of British monarchs as rulers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. The first, in 1877, proclaimed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/queen-victoria">Queen Victoria</a> Empress of India; the second, in 1903, celebrated Edward VII; and the third, in 1911, was attended in person by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-v">George V</a> and Queen Mary, who announced the reversal of the partition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> and the move of the capital from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolkata">Calcutta</a> to Delhi.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/delhi-durbar.jpg" alt="Delhi Durbar" width="320" height="178" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown photographer · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Delhi_Durbar,_1911.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The name comes from the Persian word durbar, a royal court, and the gatherings were also called Imperial Durbars. They were held at Coronation Park in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a>. At the first two the monarch was represented by the viceroy. The Proclamation Durbar, organised by Thomas Henry Thornton, opened on 1 January 1877 in the presence of Lord Lytton, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroy-of-india">Viceroy of India</a>, with maharajas, nawabs and intellectuals. It was largely an official event, and it was later criticised for drawing funds away from the Great Famine of 1876–78. At it Ganesh Vasudeo Joshi of the Poona Sarvajanik Sabha asked that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> be granted the same political and social status as was enjoyed by British subjects.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The Durbar of 1903 was planned in detail by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-curzon">Lord Curzon</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroy-of-india">Viceroy of India</a>. A deserted plain was turned into a tented city with a light railway, a post office with its own stamps, a hospital, a police force and electric light. Edward VII did not come and sent his brother, the Duke of Connaught and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strathearn">Strathearn</a>. Two weeks of processions on elephants, polo, balls and military reviews followed, and film of the event shown across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> is often credited with launching the country's early film industry.</p><p>In 1911 the King and Queen sailed from Portsmouth on RMS Medina and entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> on 7 December with 50,000 troops. The Durbar itself took place on 12 December, the King wearing the Imperial Crown of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, with 10,000 officials in a grandstand and 50,000 local people on a mound behind. The proclamation was read in English and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/urdu">Urdu</a>, and the princes paid homage. The Gaekwar of Baroda, Sayajirao III, came without his jewellery and turned his back on leaving, which was taken at the time as a sign of dissent. A review of 40,000 troops followed on 14 December.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The 1911 Durbar is generally held to have strengthened the princes' support for British rule, as their help in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> showed. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-viii">Edward VIII</a> announced in 1936 that he would hold a Durbar after his coronation, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-vi">George VI</a> at first meant to keep the plan. It was postponed in February 1937 and again in February 1938, as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-national-congress">Indian National Congress</a>, which had dominated the 1937 provincial elections in seven of eleven provinces, said it would boycott the event and refuse to vote funds for it. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> and the move towards independence ended the idea. The thrones of 1911 are shown at Rashtrapati Bhavan.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Delhi Durbar” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Under the Royal Titles Act of 1876, Victoria was proclaimed Empress of India at the <b>Delhi Durbar</b> of 1 January 1877, giving the Raj an imperial monarchy that consciously borrowed Mughal ceremony.</blockquote><a href="/empire/british-raj">British Raj · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>At the <b>Delhi Durbar</b>, attended by hundreds of thousands, King-Emperor George V announced that the capital of British India would move from Calcutta to Delhi.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-12">12 December in history</a></li></ul></section>
